<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Murcia</h1><p class="st-meta">1266 CE – 1833 CE · capital Murcia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Castilian frontier kingdom in south-eastern Spain, formed after the reconquest of Murcia and kept as a historical unit until 1833.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar-revolt-of-1264-1266">Mudéjar revolt</a> of 1264-1266, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I of Aragon</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> on behalf of his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/third-taifa-of-murcia">Third Taifa of Murcia</a>. Alfonso X resettled the territory with Castilians and Catalans and kept it as a separate kingdom within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> was a frontier facing both the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon</a>, which took its northern districts by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-torrellas">Treaty of Torrellas</a> in 1304. The irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huerta">huertas</a> produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, fruit and grain, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjar</a> population declined, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-colombia">Cartagena</a> grew into the main naval base on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The kingdom was a historical title and administrative unit rather than a self-governing state. In 1833 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javier-de-burgos">Javier de Burgos's</a> reform divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> into provinces, splitting the old kingdom into the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-albacete">Albacete</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-murcia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Murcia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Murcia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1266 CE – 1833 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Murcia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alfonso X</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Castilian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Spain</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Reino de Murcia</dd></dl></aside></div>
